Hmmm.  My (vague) memory is that the mass converted to energy is a very
 small portion of the total mass;  Let's see, cracking open the old
 Intro to Nuclear Physics (Cottingham & Greenwood), the main reactions
 are  the PPI chain  (I'm going to cheat and say the sun goes from all H
 to all He, and neglect the higher order reactions; I don't think that
 they release the same order of magnitude of energy, given relative
 abundances):

[p == Hydrogen, v == neutrino]

                 1
	p + p ->  H + e+ + v + 0.42 MeV
                 2

            2     3
        p +  H ->  He + photon + 5.49 MeV
            1     2

        3    3      4
         He   He ->  He + p + p + 12.86 MeV
        2    2      2

Net: In each reaction, 26.73 MeV per Helium formed is released, or
 6.55 MeV per hydrogen (see the neutrino energy, below).  That's
 a maximum figure for the mass, while a proton masses 938.3 MeV;

IE, max, we're losing 0.7% of the mass.

Except it's lower than this: except in the corona, some of the energy
 is not radiated out to space but heats the plasma instead (at least,
 it hasn't radiated yet at the red giant stage).  Unfortunately, I
 don't remember how much -- but the neutrinos carry away an average (still
 according to Cottingham and Greenwood) 0.26 MeV each, so that's 0.13 MeV
 per Hydrogen converted that's unconditionally lost to Out There, unless
 you've got a whole bunch of Scrith lying around.

The text I pulled out was using the numbers for an illustration of plasma
 physics reactions, so the little bit stellar of stellar mechanics is
 mine, from my memory (which has probably been more derived from Carl
 Sagan and science fiction than any hard core studies).  Still, I
 don't see how you can get a noticable mass change out of that, unless
 those lithium and upwards reactions are putting out a whole lot more
 energy than I think they are.

Anybody have any idea what the output from the solar wind is like?



